module souther.date

// Date arithmetic (ADR-0028, [#stdlib-date]). `Date` is `java.time.LocalDate`; each `let` names a
// JVM operation the backend emits, checked against the declared signature like any intrinsic. These
// are pure calendar computations, so they stay in the domain rather than becoming injected
// behaviours ([#injected-behavior]) — only reading the *current* date needs the outside world.
//
// Argument order follows F#/Elm ([#pipe]): the date operated on comes last, so
// `d |> Date.addDays(3)` reads left to right. `daysBetween(from, to)` counts whole days from `from`
// to `to`, negative when `to` precedes `from`.
let addDays (days: Int, d: Date): Date = intrinsic "date.addDays"
let addMonths (months: Int, d: Date): Date = intrinsic "date.addMonths"
let addYears (years: Int, d: Date): Date = intrinsic "date.addYears"
let daysBetween (from: Date, to: Date): Int = intrinsic "date.daysBetween"

// The parts of a date. `year` and `day` are Ints in both Elm (`Time.toYear` / `toDay`) and .NET
// (`DateTime.Year` / `Day`). `month` follows .NET rather than Elm, whose `Month` is a twelve-case
// type: `elm/time` has no month arithmetic at all, so its Month exists to be displayed and matched,
// while `addMonths` here counts in months and a named month would have to be converted back to add
// one. There is no `dayOfWeek`: neither language answers that with a bare number, so it needs a
// seven-case type in the core, and nothing yet asks for it.
//
// With `day`, the month boundary is two lines in a domain, which is where it belongs — neither Elm
// nor .NET ships a month-end function either:
//     let monthStart (d: Date): Date = Date.addDays(1 - Date.day(d), d)
//     let endOfMonth (d: Date): Date = Date.addDays(0 - 1, Date.addMonths(1, monthStart(d)))
let year (d: Date): Int = intrinsic "date.year"
let month (d: Date): Int = intrinsic "date.month"
let day (d: Date): Int = intrinsic "date.day"

// The other direction: three Ints read as a day, answering `NotADate` where they name none —
// February the 30th, month 13, a year outside what a date can hold. The inverse of the three
// readers above, so
//
//     Date.fromParts(Date.year(d), Date.month(d), Date.day(d)) == d
//
// holds for every date, which is what makes this a way back rather than a second way in that
// answers something else.
//
// A written date is refused at compile time ([#temporal-literal]) because the text is there to be
// read; parts are values, so a refusal here is a business result at the position that built them.
// That is the same split `String.toInt` makes, and for the same reason.
//
// Nothing is normalised, and that is the point of having it. The arithmetic normalises — it must,
// since shifting a day by a month has to land somewhere — so a model that reaches an impossible
// date by adding to a written one gets a real date back and no sign that it asked for a day the
// calendar does not have. Asked as three parts, the question has an answer, and it is `NotADate`.
let fromParts (year: Int, month: Int, day: Int): Date | NotADate = intrinsic "date.fromParts"
